/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The concrete non-vacuous instance of coq/SLA_Instance.v, in dL.

   AMENDED for the corrected design rule  N = 2n + 1 = d + 1  exactly
   (see ../NOTE_convex_uniqueness.md).  The instance is now

       d = 1,  N = 2  (so M = N - 1 = 1),  phi(t) = 1,  theta*_p = 1,
       z(t) = 1,      Gamma = 2,   sigma = 1,
       initial estimates  thetahat(0) = v = (0, 2),
       alpha* = (1/2, 1/2)  --  and at N = d + 1 this alpha* is UNIQUE.

   The previous version of this file used d = 1 with N = 3 and
   v = (0, 2, 1).  That is N > d + 1, the degenerate configuration: there
   the coefficients (s, s, 1 - 2s) reproduce theta* = 1 for EVERY s in
   [0, 1/2], so alpha* was not a point at all, and the second-level
   regressor E_f was rank 1 in R^2 at every t.  File 08 records both facts.

   With m^2 = 1 + phi^T phi = 2 the gradient law (4.19) reads
   thetahat_i' = -(thetahat_i - 1), whose solution is
   thetahat_i(t) = 1 + (v_i - 1) e^{-t}, i.e. t0 = 1 - e^{-t} and
   t1 = 1 + e^{-t}.  The dL model is that ODE; the exponential solution is
   never needed, because everything below is proved by differential
   induction rather than by solving.

   ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THE AMENDMENT IS WORTH FLAGGING.  In the old N = 3
   instance the last model was initialised exactly at theta*, so eps_M and
   v_f were identically zero and several statements below were trivial.
   Now the last model is index 1 with v_1 = 2, so

       eps_M(t) = eps_1(t) = e^{-t}/2  is NOT zero,
       E_f(t)   = eps_0 - eps_1 = -e^{-t}   is NOT zero,
       v_f(t)   = -(e^{-t} - e^{-2t})/2     is NOT zero,
       M_f(t)   =  (e^{-t} - e^{-2t}) ,

   and yet the residual  M_f alpha*_f + v_f  still vanishes identically
   (last entry).  That is a strictly stronger test of (4.69) than the old
   instance provided.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: hull invariance at d=1, N=2, phi=1, Gamma=2"

Description "Coq: inst_hull_invariance / inst_hull_direct.  The convex
combination (1/2, 1/2) of the two time-varying estimates is exactly
theta* = 1 at every instant.".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1), t1' = -(t1 - 1) }]
    (1/2)*t0 + (1/2)*t1 = 1
End.

Tactic "automatic ODE invariant search"
  implyR(1) ; ODE(1)
End.

End.

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: alpha* is UNIQUE at N = d+1 = 2 (Coq: inst_astar_unique)"

Description "The point of the amended design rule.  With two vertices
v = (0,2) in R^1 and target theta* = 1, the convex coefficients are forced:
the affine system has N = 2 unknowns and d+1 = 2 equations.".

ProgramVariables
  Real a0; Real a1;
End.

Problem
  \forall a0 \forall a1
  ( a0 + a1 = 1 & 0*a0 + 2*a1 = 1 -> a0 = 1/2 & a1 = 1/2 )
End.

Tactic "by QE"
  QE
End.

End.

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: non-degeneracy, and eps_M is now nonzero"

Description "Coq: inst_models_are_distinct.  Neither model sits on theta* = 1
any more: t0 stays strictly below and t1 strictly above.  In particular
eps_M = eps_1 = (t1-1)/2 > 0, unlike in the old N = 3 instance where the last
model was initialised exactly at theta*.".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1), t1' = -(t1 - 1) }]
    ( t0 < 1 & t1 > 1 & t0 < t1 )
End.

Tactic "automatic ODE invariant search"
  implyR(1) ; ODE(1)
End.

End.

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: E_f is nowhere zero (Coq: inst_E_nonzero)"

Description "E_f(t) = eps_0 - eps_1 = (t0-1)/2 - (t1-1)/2 < 0 for all t, so
the second-level regression problem is not vacuous.  At M = 1 this single
component is the whole of E_f, and being nonzero it has full rank 1 = M --
in contrast to the old N = 3 instance, where E_f was rank 1 in R^2.".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1), t1' = -(t1 - 1) }]
    (t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2 < 0
End.

Tactic "automatic ODE invariant search"
  implyR(1) ; ODE(1)
End.

End.

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: 4.51  E_f alpha*_f = -eps_M  (M=1)"

Description "Coq: inst_Ealp / E_alpha_star_exact.  Now a real identity rather
than 0 = 0: both sides are nonzero, and they agree because theta* is in the
hull (e_alpha = 0).".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1), t1' = -(t1 - 1) }]
    ( (t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2 ) * (1/2) = -((t1 - 1)/2)
End.

Tactic "hull invariant, then arithmetic"
  implyR(1) ; dC("t0 + t1 = 2", 1) ; <(
    dW(1) ; QE,
    ODE(1)
  )
End.

End.

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   (4.66) and (4.69) for the instance, with M_f and v_f carried as genuine
   state variables:

       M_f' = -sigma M_f + E_f^T E_f ,     M_f(0) = 0
       v_f' = -sigma v_f + E_f^T eps_M ,   v_f(0) = 0

   at sigma = 1, M = 1, with E_f = (t0-1)/2 - (t1-1)/2 and eps_M = (t1-1)/2.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: M_f >= 0 along (4.66) (Coq: Mf_psd)"

Description "M_f is a 1x1 matrix here, so positive semidefiniteness is just
M_f >= 0; it follows by Darboux with cofactor -sigma = -1.".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1; Real mf;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2 & mf = 0
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1),
     t1' = -(t1 - 1),
     mf' = -mf + ((t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2)*((t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2) }]
    mf >= 0
End.

Tactic "Darboux inequality with cofactor -sigma"
  implyR(1) ; dbx("-1", 1)
End.

End.

ArchiveEntry "SLA/instance: the residual M_f alpha* + v_f vanishes, with v_f nonzero"

Description "Coq: qres_zero / inst_MFalp.  The sharper test the amended
instance makes possible: M_f(t) = e^{-t} - e^{-2t} and
v_f(t) = -(e^{-t} - e^{-2t})/2 are both nonzero, yet the residual
M_f alpha*_0 + v_f = M_f/2 + v_f is identically zero.  (4.69) omits the v_f
term; this entry is exactly the situation where that omission would matter.".

ProgramVariables
  Real t0; Real t1; Real mf; Real vf;
End.

Problem
  t0 = 0 & t1 = 2 & mf = 0 & vf = 0
 ->
  [{ t0' = -(t0 - 1),
     t1' = -(t1 - 1),
     mf' = -mf + ((t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2)*((t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2),
     vf' = -vf + ((t0 - 1)/2 - (t1 - 1)/2)*((t1 - 1)/2) }]
    mf*(1/2) + vf = 0
End.

Tactic "hull invariant, then Darboux on the residual"
  implyR(1) ; dC("t0 + t1 = 2", 1) ; <(
    ODE(1),
    ODE(1)
  )
End.

End.
